{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791130153,"nonce":"OIN2w3eiWLJU+8nETIhQyq/hBRw=","title":"Blinc's baseline pass moves InlineFlow's absolutely positioned pieces","message":"Since the Blinc bump to deeeec98 (53663b6), LayoutTree::align_baselines shifts every child of a row with align-items: baseline, including absolutely positioned ones. InlineFlow places its text pieces and inline boxes absolutely, with explicit left and top, inside p and code, which the user-agent sheet makes baseline rows. So they get a baseline shift on top of the position InlineFlow computed.\n\nSmoke: \"an inline box in the flow, as code is, has its text on the line's baseline\" fails, [139.912, 138.912]. It passed at Blinc 6accf69.\n\nIn CSS, absolutely positioned children of a flex container are not flex items and take no part in baseline alignment. Reported to the Blinc session: skip position: absolute children in aligns_to_baseline. Then bump the pin and rerun the smoke test.","files":null}]}